// Labelled verification cases — one JSON object per line.
//
// §19 asks for claim accuracy, evidence coverage, unsupported-claim rate,
// contradiction-detection rate and false-verification rate. This file is the
// labelled set those are measured on: each case is a claim, the evidence offered
// for it, and the verdict a correct verification stage must reach.
//
// SYNTHETIC, and deliberately so. These are not sampled from production traffic —
// they are constructed failures, one per way a claim can be wrong. That makes the
// set a regression guard rather than an estimate of real-world rates, and it
// should be described that way wherever the numbers appear. Real traffic will
// have a different mix and will need its own labels.
//
// Fields:
//   id, claim, expect, category, why
//   evidence[]: {uri, summary, tier, kind, stance, age_days}
//     tier   : primary | official | community | unverified
//     kind   : document | on_chain_metric | market_data | statistical_signal
//              | security_advisory | incident_report | audit_finding
//     stance : supports | contradicts | neutral
//     age_days: how old the evidence is; drives the temporal check
//
// The `expect` values are §13's four statuses. `verified` means a correct system
// accepts the claim; everything else means it must not.

